3.1 Verbatim copies kept outside kernel/
----------------------------------------

`facade/hll_int.h` -- `$RSRC/src/hyperloglog.c:183-207` (struct hllhdr, the
HLL_*_CACHE / HLL_P / HLL_REGISTERS / HLL_BITS / HLL_*_ENCODING constants) and
`:354-406` (HLL_DENSE_GET_REGISTER, HLL_DENSE_SET_REGISTER, the HLL_SPARSE_*
opcodes, HLL_ALPHA_INF). Upstream keeps them file-local and has no
hyperloglog.h, but the facade needs them: PFDEBUG GETREG reads registers with
the dense macro, PFCOUNT assembles the 8-byte cached cardinality, PFSELFTEST
drives the low-level register test.

Copied lines were checked byte-for-byte against `$RSRC` (comment prose trimmed,
67 code lines compared, no difference). This is the one place where the layout
is duplicated, so it is also the one place where a silent drift would turn into
corrupted HLL bytes instead of a compile error: `tools/redis-diff` (Task 10)
compares a library-produced value with a real Redis one to cover it.

Not copied on purpose: `invalid_hll_err` (a file-scope variable, re-declared by
each consumer with the leading '-' that addReplyError() strips removed), the
`simd_enabled` switch and the AVX2/NEON helpers (a redislib build defines
neither HAVE_AVX2 nor HAVE_AARCH64_NEON, so the kernel compiles the scalar
paths).

Same technique, earlier: `kernel/t_zset.h` is the sorted-set/skiplist type and
API block lifted verbatim out of `server.h` (see section 3), and
`facade/zset_int.h` (§3.2) is the second copy made the same way.

3.2 Verbatim copy #2 -- facade/zset_int.h
-----------------------------------------

`facade/zset_int.h` -- `$RSRC/src/t_zset.c:1950-2014` (the `zsetopsrc` and
`zsetopval` iterator types with their `iterset` / `iterzset` unions) and
`:2301-2334` (`REDIS_AGGR_SUM|MIN|MAX|COUNT`, `zuiWeightedScore()`,
`zunionInterAggregate()`), copied from this port's own `kernel/t_zset.c`, where
those lines are byte-identical to `$RSRC`. Both ranges were re-extracted by
script and compared back: 65 + 34 lines, no difference.

Why: ZUNION / ZINTER / ZDIFF iterate their operands through the `zui*()` API,
which takes those two types, and they aggregate through those two functions. All
of it is file-scope in t_zset.c (upstream declares the types and the
`REDIS_AGGR_*` macros in server.h, which is not part of this port), so a facade
cannot reach them.

Deliberately NOT copied: the ZDIFF algorithm block (`zdiffAlgorithm1/2`,
`zsetChooseDiffAlgorithm`, `zdiff`) and the `zsetRemoveFromSkiplist()` it calls.
That chain reaches the file-local skiplist internals (`zslDelete()` and its
helpers), and copying those would move the skiplist itself into the facade.
`facade/sortedset.c` runs upstream's algorithm 2 -- stage the first operand,
delete every later operand's members, insert what survives -- against the
exported `dict*()` / `zslInsert()` API instead. The result set is the same; the
heuristic that picks between upstream's two algorithms is not ported because the
facade only implements the one.

Also authored for the port, in the facade layer: `rl_common.{c,h}` (the object
model and the RESP->Lua reply helpers, including `rl_pushdouble()` and
`rl_free_zset()` contributed by Task 4) and the four command facades. Their
reply-mapping and no-keyspace deviations are marked `rl:` at the site of each
one, and section 6 lists them per command family.

Files the brief listed that are NOT in `kernel/`: none. Files beyond the brief's
list: `endianconv.c`, `monotonic.c`, `mt19937-64.{c,h}`, `sha256.{c,h}`,
`fpconv_dtoa.{c,h}`, `fpconv_powers.h`, `hyperloglog.h`, `t_zset.h`, `config.h`,
`fmacros.h`, `debugmacro.h`, `listpack.{c,h}`, `listpack_malloc.h` -- all of them
are in the dependency closure of the required kernels: `util.c` needs
fpconv/monotonic/sha256/endianconv, `dict.h:45` includes mt19937-64.h because
`dict.h:242` defines `randomULong()` as `genrand64_int64()`, `sds.c` needs
sdsalloc/config, `geohash_helper.c` needs debugmacro.h, `t_zset.c` needs listpack
for the listpack encoding of a zset (added while finishing Task 1, so that the
whole kernel -- including t_zset.c and bitops.c -- compiles and links), and the
remaining `#include "config.h"` / `"fmacros.h"` lines are preserved by edit A.


