5. Verification
---------------

Line-ending note for every byte comparison below: the project default is
`* text=auto`, which would hand a Windows checkout CRLF files while `$RSRC` is
LF. `.gitattributes` pins `redislib-src/**` and the two redislib test files to
`eol=lf` so the comparison works in any checkout; if a comparison ever reports
*every* line changed, that is a line-ending artefact -- strip the CR
(`sed 's/\r$//'`) before believing it.

Build shape -- one `rlcore` library. `integration/redislib.mk` links the 21
kernel objects into a single shared library, `rlcore.dll` (plus its MinGW import
library `librlcore.a`) on Windows and `librlcore.so` on Linux, installed in the
output root next to `skynet.exe` / `skynet`. The four `luaclib/redis/*.so`
modules import their kernel calls from it instead of each carrying a private
copy of the whole kernel: on Windows the four modules were 4,657,995 bytes
together and are now 1,703,832 -- a 1,060,668-byte `rlcore.dll` plus 108-209 KB
per module (on Linux, 763,304 + 478,672 bytes). The size
was never the point though -- four copies were four `server` structs, four dict
siphash seeds and four RNG streams, and the Windows stand-in for `random()`
starts from a hardcoded constant, so two modules drew the *same* sequence (for
skiplist levels and for dict sampling alike). Now the process has one of each.

Only function calls cross that boundary. `nm` over the facade objects showed
exactly three globals they read directly -- `server`, `hashDictType`,
`sdsReplyDictType` -- and all three are defined by the authored shim rather than
by a verbatim kernel file, so each became an accessor (section 4). Taking the
*address* of data living in another Windows DLL is settled by the linker's
auto-import rewriting rather than by the import table, and this port does not
want its correctness to rest on that: `objdump -p` over the shipped modules now
lists kernel functions -- plus those accessors, in the two modules that call
them -- under `DLL Name: rlcore.dll`, and no data symbol at all. Re-verified
after the split: the three seeds of the first run replayed unchanged (424242 /
4000, 777 / 4000, 20261010 / 6000 -- 0 unexplained differences, and the same
`no-command-table` buckets, 11 / 8 / 18), three fresh seeds replayed the same way
(1 / 4000, 2 / 4000, 3 / 6000), `interop.sh` 15/15, and the one-shot suite green
on both platforms in both `--redislib` and `--no-redislib` mode.

`tools/redislib-harness.c` is a pure-C assertion program over the ported kernels
(geohash encode/decode round trip and the distance helpers, HLL 10k cardinality
inside +/-2% plus sparse->dense and hllMerge, dict insert/rehash/lookup/delete
with siphash, sds, util string<->number, intset encoding upgrades, adlist,
bitops popcount/bitpos/bitfield/overflow, and the zset kernel: skiplist add/
update/conditions/del, all rank orderings, listpack<->skiplist conversion
round trip, zsetDup, direct zsl API). It ends with:

    redislib harness OK: hll=9973

Build and run it with a native Linux gcc from the Windows side:

    MSYS_NO_PATHCONV=1 MSYS2_ARG_CONV_EXCL='*' \
      wsl -d Ubuntu-24.04 -- bash /mnt/e/git/skynetjit/tools/run-harness-wsl.sh

The runner compiles every `.c` in `kernel/` (all 21, including the newly added
listpack.c) plus the harness. gcc 13.3.0 build is warning-clean apart from 8
"defined but not used" notices for the static `zsl*`/`zzl*`/`zdiff` helpers kept
on purpose (section 2). `-Wno-unused-variable` and `-Wno-maybe-uninitialized`
are passed by the runner; see its header comment for the reasons.

`hll=9973` is the expected estimate for 10000 distinct elements at the default
HLL precision (Redis' own standard error is 0.81%), and it is deterministic
because the HLL hash is siphash with a randomised dict seed only for `dict`, not
for HLL.

Runtime side, per facade task: `tests/redislib_smoke.lua` (started by
`tests/redislib-config.lua` inside the built tree) drives the real Lua modules
through skynet -- bitmap since Task 2, hll since Task 3, each asserting the
Redis reply shapes and the upstream error texts. It is green on both platforms:
`build.sh` on Windows and `build-linux.sh` under WSL Ubuntu 24.04 print
`redislib-smoke: redislib modules succeeded`.

Public-surface enumeration (2026-10-11). The callable names are read off the
built tree, not off the source registration tables: a probe service started
inside `build/out` requires each of the four modules, `pairs()` its module table
and the class metatable of a throwaway handle, and writes the sorted names out.
What the built tree answers: bitmap 7 commands (6 with a handle form, BITOP
module-only) + `dump/free/len`; hll 5, all with a handle form, plus the
multi-object module shapes of PFCOUNT/PFMERGE/PFSELFTEST; sortedset 31 -- 26
names have a handle form, 5 (ZUNION / ZINTER / ZDIFF / ZINTERCARD / ZMPOP) are
module functions only, and the 4 STORE-family names are valid both ways because
their destination is the receiver; geo 10, all with a handle form, plus
`geo.geosearchstore(dst, src, ...)`. 53 in total, each with its upper-case alias
on the table it lives on.

That enumeration is what caught a name registered where it can never work:
`zmpop` sat in `zset_methods` too, and since it reads its operand list as
argument 1, `handle:zmpop()` could only fail ("table expected, got userdata").
The class registration is gone; `tests/redislib_smoke.lua` now asserts both
halves of the shape (`handle.zmpop == nil and handle.ZMPOP == nil`,
`sortedset.zmpop ~= nil and sortedset.ZMPOP ~= nil`), and section 6 records
ZMPOP as module-form-only. Re-verified after that change: `build.sh --no-sync`
and `build-linux.sh --no-sync` both `Tests passed`, and differential seed 424242
(4000 ops) still reports 0 unexplained differences with the same
`no-command-table` bucket count (11).

Argument-shape sweep (2026-10-11, the same enumeration extended). Calling every
published name for real is not only a way to find unreachable names: it also
shows what each one answers when it is handed too little. The probe calls every
class name on a fresh handle with no arguments, every module name the same way,
every name again on a handle that has been `free()`d, and every `load()` with
400 mutated or truncated copies of a valid blob. It found, and this tree now
