Differential check against a live server (`tools/redis-diff`, manual by design --
it needs a second build and a running process, so it is not in the one-shot test):
`gen.lua` writes a deterministic operation log, `replay_redis.py` runs it against
`redis-server` 8.10.0 built from `$RSRC` over a bare RESP2 socket, `replay_lib.lua`
runs the same log through the modules inside skynet, and `diff.py` compares the two
recordings. Each step also snapshots the *state* of the keys it touched with
read-only commands both sides have (`GET`/`BITCOUNT`, `PFCOUNT`,
`ZRANGE 0 -1 WITHSCORES`), as hex, so a command whose reply is deliberately shaped
differently here (BITOP answers a handle, ZMPOP answers a handle where Redis answers
a key name) is still compared through what it left behind. First run: 4000 ops,
53 commands, 1721 state probes, 0 unexplained differences and 9 that are the two
documented deviations in section 6; `interop.sh` adds the spec §4 round trip in both
directions, 15 checks. Five real defects came out of it -- `ZRANK` and counted
`ZRANDMEMBER` crashing on a handle that emptied out, three `BITPOS` replies that
were nil where Redis always answers a number, `PFMERGE` losing the destination's own
registers, and the search family raising where Redis answers the empty array -- all
described with their evidence in `tools/redis-diff/REPORT-2026-10-10.md`.

Same oplog, older server (2026-10-11). Seed 900001 / 4000 ops was replayed
against a local `redis-server` **7.0.12** as well (Windows build, in its own
empty db1; the harness refuses to flush a non-empty database -- see
`tools/redis-diff`), and the library side ran on the identical oplog, confirmed
by md5. That comparison shows 735 differing records, and every one of them is a
version gap rather than a port gap:
`BITOP DIFF/DIFF1/ANDOR/ONE` are Redis 8 operations, so the 7.0.12 side answers
`ERR syntax error` on the 42 ops that use them and never creates those
destination keys -- every later probe on those keys is then cascade, which is
what the 611 bitmap diffs are; the remaining 124 are the GEOSEARCH/GEORADIUS
family's `ASC` ordering, which 7.0.12 resolves differently from 8.10.0. The
sorted-set and HLL families showed no difference at all against 7.0.12. The
control that settles it: the *same* oplog against the port's real target,
8.10.0, reports 0 unexplained differences. So this library is byte- and
reply-compatible with Redis 8.10; behaviour that changed between 7.0 and 8.x is
outside the contract, and a client migrating from 7.x gains the four BITOP
operators rather than losing anything.

Scope note: the harness is a Linux-native-gcc program (WSL Ubuntu 24.04, gcc
13.3.0).

Windows (MinGW-w64 gcc 16) compiles the whole kernel and both shipped facades
with zero warnings now. The four portability problems an early per-file syntax
scan reported are fixed at shim/flag level, and none of them touched an
algorithm line:

    sha256.h  `typedef uint32_t WORD` collides with windows.h' WORD
              -> guarded with `#ifndef _WINDEF_` (section 4)
    util.c    mkdir(dname, 0755)  -- MSVCRT declares mkdir with one argument
    util.c    redis_fsync(fd)     -- no fsync(), _commit() is the equivalent
              -> the `#if defined(_WIN32)` mapping block in redislib_port.h
    RL_CFLAGS carried the repo's WIN_FORCE (compat.h -> windows.h), which is what
              produced the WORD clash in the first place -> removed from
              RL_CFLAGS; the kernel is plain C and needs no Lua compat headers

Two more warning classes are suppressed for the kernel objects only, by
`integration/redislib.mk`; the facade rule keeps the full `-Wall`:

    -Wno-pointer-to-int-cast  LLP64 (`long` is 32-bit) warns on upstream's
              pointer casts -- the alignment masks in bitops.c, the pointer terms
              of dictFingerprint() in dict.c, lpRepr() in listpack.c. Each keeps
              the bits it masks or feeds a debug-only fingerprint, so no
              behaviour depends on the width; rewriting them would put
              non-upstream casts inside verbatim kernel files.
    -Wno-unused-variable      file-scope symbols whose only consumers were the
              command layer deleted by edit B, e.g. hyperloglog.c's
              invalid_hll_err (the facades re-declare the text).


