6. Deviations from a live Redis, by command family
---------------------------------------------------

Every one of these is also marked `rl:` at its site in the facade source. They
are the complete list of places where a redislib command cannot be byte-for-byte
the same program as the Redis command, and they are what a reviewer should
compare against `$RSRC` instead of expecting equality.

Object model (all families): there is no keyspace, no client, no DB and no
protocol. A command's key argument becomes the handle the method is called on,
`addReply*` becomes the Lua reply (spec §3.2), `server.dirty` /
`notifyKeyspaceEvent` / AOF-and-replica propagation are absent, and an object
that empties out stays addressable instead of being deleted (so `zcard()` after
`zrem()`-ing everything replies 0 where a live server replies 0 for a missing
key too, and `zadd()` after that starts from empty in both).

Bitmap: none beyond the object model. BITFIELD's offset cap is Redis' own
`proto-max-bulk-len` default (512MB), taken as a constant because there is no
config to read.

One difference is not the kernel's but Lua's, and it bites at 2^53: a BITFIELD
`i64`/wide-`u*` value can exceed what a LuaJIT number (a double, Lua 5.1 numeric
model) represents exactly, and it does on both sides of the call. Writing
`b:bitfield("set","i64",0,4611686018427387907)` stores 2^62, not 2^62+3, because
the literal is already rounded before the facade sees it -- measured against a
live server, Redis holds `4000000000000003` where the handle holds
`4000000000000000`. Building the same pattern out of two exactly representable
halves (`u32` at 0 = 2^30, `u32` at 32 = 3) produces byte-for-byte the image the
server holds, so the kernel and the bit layout are exact and only the number
surface rounds. Reading a wide field back rounds the same way. Callers who need
the full 64-bit range must split the field or keep the value in `dump()`/`load()`
bytes; `tools/redis-diff` compares such replies numerically, which is why this
shows up as agreement there.

One known, bounded leak: `bitmap_bitfield_generic()` keeps the ops
array it is growing while it parses, and Lua errors `longjmp` out with no chance to
free, so a malformed `BITFIELD` call that fails *after* an op was already parsed
leaks up to ~32 bytes per op parsed so far (worst case a few hundred KB, once per
bad call, never on a successful one). Removing it means turning the op parser into
non-raising helpers -- a restructure of upstream's shape, not a mechanical edit --
so it is recorded here instead. Every *other* facade site was made leak-free by
validating before allocating: `BITOP` checks its operand kinds first, `ZADD` and
`GEOADD` pre-validate scores and element types, `PFADD` walks its operands on the
Lua stack, and the store commands' work areas are only allocated once nothing left
in the function can raise.

HyperLogLog: PFDEBUG SIMD has no runtime switch (this build defines neither
HAVE_AVX2 nor HAVE_AARCH64_NEON), so it always answers "disabled", which is what
a scalar Redis build answers; and a bad ON/OFF argument raises instead of
replying the error and then the status the way upstream's fall-through does,
because one Lua call returns one value. PFCOUNT's cached-cardinality write-back
does not mark anything dirty. `createHLLObject()`'s robj is a view the facade
discards; kernel calls that may replace `o->ptr` are handed a stack robj and the
sds is written back after every call.

Sorted set: range bounds are parsed by the facade with the same
`fast_float_strtod()` + rejection rules as the file-local `zslParseRange()`
(which is why a score of "1e400" is refused while a bound of "1e400" is not --
upstream uses `string2d()` for scores and `fast_float_strtod()` for bounds);
range *deletion* walks the range and calls `zsetDel()` per member instead of the
file-local `zsl/zzlDeleteRangeBy*()` helpers, so it is O(M log N) rather than
O(M + log N) on a skiplist; ZDIFF has only upstream's algorithm 2, not the
1-versus-2 cost heuristic; ZMPOP's reply carries the source handle where Redis
carries the key name, and ZMPOP is a module function only (`sortedset.zmpop`,
never `handle:zmpop`) because its operand list is argument 1 and a receiver
would have nothing to be; ZUNION/ZINTER/ZDIFF/ZINTERCARD take their key list as
one array table (Redis' `numkeys` integer has nothing to count here), while the
STORE forms stay methods because their destination is the receiver.
Scores are Lua numbers in both directions (spec §3.1's type principle); on the
wire Redis sends a double as a bulk string, so `tools/redis-diff` compares them
numerically.

Sorted set, in the wrapper: the `BYSCORE REV` case takes its bounds as
[max,min] because that is what upstream does -- `zrangeGenericCommand()` swaps
the two argument indexes whenever REV is combined with BYSCORE or BYLEX, which
is why Redis' own documented form is `ZRANGE key +inf -inf BYSCORE REV`. A test
written as `zrange(1, 3, "byscore", "rev")` gets an empty reply here *and* on a
real server.

Geo: `GEOADD` reads CH itself (upstream leaves CH to the `zaddCommand()` it
re-dispatches to, and there is no re-dispatch here); the option words are still
accepted in the same positions, and the reply rule is upstream's -- `CH` makes
it count updates as well as additions. `STORE` / `STOREDIST` name a handle
instead of a key, and that handle may be a geoset or a plain sorted set, which
is the type Redis writes there. `WITHHASH` truncates to an integer like
`addReplyLongLong()`, so with a STOREDIST-written target (whose scores are
distances, not geohashes) it reports the truncated distance, exactly as the
server does. Distances are the value Redis *renders* -- four decimals, then read
back as a number -- not the full-precision double, so a client switching between
a real server and this library sees the same quantity.
Searching from exactly +180 longitude finds only the points east of the
antimeridian, even with an 5000 km radius: `geohashCalculateAreasByShapeWGS84()`
saturates the box at +180 and the neighbour boxes never wrap. That is upstream's
behaviour (the kernel is verbatim), not the port's, and the smoke test asserts
it on purpose: a search from -180, and one from a member at -179.99, do find
both sides.

Dump and load (spec §4; not Redis commands, so this is the one place where a
redislib value crosses a process boundary): a bitmap's or an HLL's `dump()` is
the Redis *string value* itself -- byte for byte what `GET` of such a key
returns, sparse or dense -- and `load()` is `SET` plus, for an HLL, the same
read-in check every PF* command makes (`isHLLObjectOrReply()`'s data side, so a
blob that is too short, mis-magicked, has an unknown encoding byte, or is dense
with the wrong length gets `WRONGTYPE Key is not a valid HyperLogLog string
value.`). Because PFCOUNT caches its estimate inside the value, as the server
does, a `dump()` taken after a count differs by those 8 bytes from one taken
before it -- the same two sketches converge once both have been counted, which
the smoke test asserts, and `tools/redis-diff/interop.sh` asserts against a live
server. The sorted-set family uses the small non-RDB layout spec §4 defines
(`uint32 count + (float64 score, uint32 len, bytes member)*`, integers written
with shifts so the blob is little-endian on any host, records in ascending score
order so one set always writes one blob). It is deliberately not Redis' DUMP
format, which is RDB plus a CRC64 trailer and is not what "a skynet service can
store this in any medium" needs; a blob is replayed against a real server with
ZADD / GEOADD instead, and the smoke test hand-decodes one to prove no reader
needs a Redis library. Loading validates the shape strictly -- a count that
cannot be filled from the remaining bytes is refused *before* the dictionary is
sized from it, and trailing bytes, truncation, and a NaN score all raise
`Bad data format`, upstream's own reply for a serialized payload it cannot parse
(`cluster.c:290`). `+inf` and `-inf` are legal scores there because ZADD accepts
them.

Two more classes of difference are visible from a live server and were confirmed
by `tools/redis-diff` rather than reasoned about in advance. Both sides still
answer the same *shape*; only the text or the precondition differs:

`no-command-table` -- Redis rejects a command that is too short from the `arity`
field of its command table, before the handler ever runs. This port has no command
table, so the handler's own complaint is what surfaces: `GEOSEARCH key
FROMLONLAT 15 37` (no BYRADIUS/BYBOX) gets
`ERR wrong number of arguments for 'geosearch' command` from a server and
`ERR exactly one of BYRADIUS and BYBOX can be specified for geosearch` here. Both
are Redis' own sentences; which of the two fires is the difference. The same
covers an argument that is not passed at all where a *value* was expected:
`ZRANK key` gets arity from a server, `handle:zrank()` gets
`"string or number expected, got no value"` here -- see section 7 for why the
presence-only arguments were fixed and these were not.

`no-keyspace` -- a command that looks its key up on the server can be told the key
is not there, and says so: `PFDEBUG todense missingkey` replies
`ERR The specified key does not exist`. There is no such lookup here -- the object
is the receiver -- so the command runs on the untouched handle. The same fact,
*applied* where it changes an answer rather than an error, is the rule the search
commands follow now: `GEORADIUSBYMEMBER`/`GEOSEARCH FROMMEMBER` of an unresolvable
member raise on a server only when the source key exists, and reply the empty
array (0 and a cleared destination with `STORE`) when it does not, so an empty
handle takes the latter route. `ZRANK`, `ZRANDMEMBER <count>` and `BITPOS` on an
emptied handle take their server counterparts' answers for the same reason; see
`tools/redis-diff/REPORT-2026-10-10.md` for how each was found.

7. Error replies: the code a client sees
-----------------------------------------

`rl_error()` (facade/rl_common.c) is the port of `addReplyErrorLength()`
(networking.c:657), and it is the only place that decides how an error reads:

    if (!len || s[0] != '-') addReplyProto(c,"-ERR ",5);

Upstream's rule is that a message carrying its own error code writes the code
into the string with a leading dash -- `"-WRONGTYPE Key is not a valid
HyperLogLog string value."` (hyperloglog.c:1636), `"-INVALIDOBJ Corrupted HLL
object detected"` (hyperloglog.c:208), `shared.syntaxerr` = `"-ERR syntax
error\r\n"` (server.c:2225) -- and every other message gets the generic code.
So the text a client actually reads is `WRONGTYPE ...` for the first group and
`ERR syntax error`, `ERR need numeric radius`, `ERR value is not a valid float`
for the second.

The facade call sites keep upstream's literals *including* the leading dash, and
`rl_error()` applies the one rule, so a Lua caller of any redislib command sees
exactly the text a Redis client sees for the same failure -- which is what makes
switching a call site from `skynet.db.redis` to these modules a non-event for
error handling. Formatting goes through `sdscatvprintf()`, the same call
`addReplyErrorFormatInternal()` uses, so `"%f"` renders identically (`invalid
longitude,latitude pair 200.000000,37.000000`). LuaJIT still prepends its usual
`file:line: ` position to the raised message, like any other Lua error; that is
the one difference from a RESP client, and `errtext()` in the smoke test strips
it.

Three texts are the facade's own, because Redis has no equivalent failure:
`"string or number expected, got %s"` (a Lua argument can be a table or a
handle, a RESP argument cannot), `"string expected, got %s"` (the same, for
`load()`, where a number must not be coerced because the argument is a byte
string), and `"table expected, got %s"` (the same, for the operand list the
multi-object commands take). Upstream cannot express any of them -- every
element of `c->argv` is already a string there -- so these read as Lua errors
with the generic code in front of them.

The rule these three do NOT cover, and the reason they are the only three: a
`luaL_checkstring()`/"bad argument" message used to reach callers wherever the
code needed an argument to *be there* -- a subcommand or option word, an operand
list, a STORE form's destination, a GEORADIUS unit. "bad argument #N to '?'" is
not a sentence any Redis client can be handed, and the `?` could not even name
the command. Those sites now go through `rl_checklist()` / `rl_argstring()`:
absence answers with the server's own line (`"wrong number of arguments for '%s'
command"`, verbatim from the arity check at `server.c:2225`, and confirmed
against a live 8.10.0 for each shape), while an argument of the wrong kind
answers with one of the three texts above.

What that does *not* claim, stated because the difference is visible: an
argument read as a value (`rl_member()`, `rl_checklong()`, the float readers --
51 call sites across the four facades) is still refused by the value parser
first, so a missing member replies
`"string or number expected, got no value"` where a server would have stopped at
its command table and said `wrong number of arguments for 'zrank' command`.
Making that side match too means passing the command name to every value reader
and deciding, per command, which arguments are required: a separate change of
some size, not part of this one. It is recorded as a deviation in section 6
(`no-command-table`), and `tools/redis-diff` cannot see it because every
operation in the generated log is well-formed.
`tests/redislib_smoke.lua`'s `missing-args` family pins the three outcomes this
rule does cover: arity for absence, a facade text for the wrong kind, and
`WRONGTYPE` for a handle of the wrong kind -- the last being exactly what a
server says in that case.

That claim is checked, not asserted: a scan of the facades' 119 `rl_error(L,
"...")` sites against `$RSRC` finds 116 whose literal appears verbatim in the
pristine tree (allowing for the `\r\n` that the `shared.*` error objects carry),
and the 3 remaining ones are the sentences above. The arity messages go through
`'%s' command` with the name passed as an argument for exactly that reason: no
facade literal is a paraphrase of a Redis message. `grep` over `facade/*.c`
finds no remaining raw `luaL_check*` call, which is what makes "no `bad
argument` text can reach a caller" a property of the code rather than of the
cases one probe happened to try.

The Lua wrappers (Task 7) raise their own texts on purpose, before anything
reaches C: `zadd element 1 must be {score, member}`, `geoadd takes the options
before the element list`, `zrange: unknown option bogus`. Those are complaints
about the *table shapes* spec §3.1 introduced, which Redis has no syntax for and
therefore no message for; a Redis-format failure still comes out of the C layer
with the text above.
